---
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# agent.md.example
# This is the template for an agent. Agents are named personas —
# system prompts for a specific role (reviewer, refactorer, etc.).
#
# Copy this folder to agents/<your-agent-name>/agent.md, edit, then run
# `make register` to update manifest.json.
#
# Agents live in DIRECTORIES because they may ship adjacent material
# (eval.json, prompts, references) the same way skills do.
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

name: example-agent
description: One-line description of when to invoke this agent.
author: your-name

presets:
  - skill-development

# Optional allowlist of tools that should install this agent.
tools:
  - claude-code
  - cursor
  - vscode-copilot

# Optional per-tool frontmatter overrides. Common keys per tool:
overrides:
  cursor:
    # Cursor subagents: see https://cursor.com/docs/subagents
    model: inherit          # or a specific model id
    readonly: false         # set true to deny write tools
    is_background: false    # set true for parallel background work
  vscode-copilot:
    # VS Code Copilot custom agents: see
    # https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/cloud-agent/about-custom-agents
    tools:                  # restrict the tools this agent can call
      - codebase
      - githubRepo
    model: claude-sonnet-4-6
    # mcp-servers:          # optional MCP server scoping
    #   - name: my-mcp
---

# Example Agent

The body of this file is the agent's system prompt. Write it as
direct instructions to the model.

## When to use this agent

A clear trigger. "Invoke this agent when you have a design document,
RFC, or substantive code change that affects the shape of a system."

## When not to use it

When the work doesn't match the trigger, the agent should bow out.
"For implementation-level review of a well-scoped change, use a code
reviewer instead, not this one."

## How to brief the agent

Tell the agent what it should ask the user for, and what it should
read up front. This is the section that turns a vague invocation into
a focused conversation.

1. What context to expect.
2. What constraints to honour.
3. What's out of scope.

## What good output looks like

Be explicit about the response shape. Bulleted findings? A diff
proposal? A short verdict line? Skills and agents that don't describe
their own output drift quickly.

## Hard rules

- Never silently rewrite the user's files. Show diffs first.
- Never editorialise without grounding — cite frames / lines / functions.
- Stop when uncertain rather than guessing.

## Gotchas

- **Body IS the system prompt.** All agent-supporting tools (Claude
  Code, Cursor subagents, Copilot custom agents) read the file body
  as the agent's instructions. Frontmatter is metadata; the body is
  what the model sees.
- **Cursor reads `.cursor/agents/`, `.claude/agents/`, and `.codex/
  agents/` all together.** Installing the same agent for both Claude
  Code and Cursor lands it in two paths; Cursor sees both. Harmless.
- **VS Code Copilot custom agents vs chatmodes.** The toolkit
  generates `.github/agents/<name>.md` (custom agents). Chat modes
  (`.chatmode.md`) are a different mechanism we intentionally don't
  generate; hand-craft them if you need them.
- **`overrides.<tool>` keys are tool-specific.** Cursor cares about
  `model`/`readonly`/`is_background`; Copilot cares about
  `tools`/`model`/`mcp-servers`. Setting a Copilot-only key under
  `overrides.cursor` is a no-op (Cursor ignores it) but won't error.

